ol or for assessing the  situation and acting appropriately  rather it was repeatedly asked described  as a suicide-by-cop attempt and that the  police officer didn't fall for it as  though the police's task were to fight  against the passive inevitability of  being the instrumentality of the  subjects will a hero this police officer  was described for resisting the  invitation to shoot the man he didn't  let the suspect turn his hand into a  lethal weapon he refused to let the  suspect rest bullets from his gun he  didn't obey when the suspect ordered him  to kill and the agency here is weird and  I think of it as I tend to call it the  ventriloquism of the passive voice the  passive voice is used to bury the agency  and to reassign responsibilities as  sometimes in these situations the active  voice is used to animate the passive but  this is a species of puppetry that  puppetry that short-circuits painful yet  crucial opportunities to reflect upon  the complexities of power and its  absolutes and I worry we have dulled our  responses to the sharp moral questions  we might otherwise  have to confront in recent years again  an increasingly increasing number of  such confrontations are called police  assisted suicide or against suicide by  cop  and this unfortunate terminology shifts  agency away from a public officer who  justifiably or not actually makes the  decision to fire his weapon it read  ascribes the police as passive  extensions of the outlandish will of the  deceased by law police actions are  constrained by the reasonable exercise  of force but why bother with all that if  dying was by the crazy person's choice  rather than at the officers hand it is  the sort of shifted responsibility you  used to hear in fiction that used to  come out of the Wild West the frontier  West when people some people would say  he needed killin it used to be that kind  of cliche common perhaps as a  regionalism of the south used as a  cipher for vigilante justice it referred  casually to extra legal justice or an  impassioned response to a particularly  heinous crime it meant that someone or  maybe a group of vigilantes took it upon  themselves to do away with a dastardly  criminal without due process without  benefit of jury without benefit of doubt  or appeal because it is a mark that does  not have the right to have rights as  political scientists Margaret Summers  might put it now this is also pay to  play and a consequence of narrow  cost-benefit thinking is that the  exchange not only is deadly  but cheapens us the polity the state  I've written before about the serial  killer in the Bay Area a man who  strangled his victims and convicted and  sentenced to death he begged the state  to hang him he apparently got as much  erotic pleasure from strangulation as  Ted Nugent gets from machine gunning  what a peculiar moral lens we are left  with if we view executions as choice as  preference when we over endow prisoners  with an autonomy far beyond the  constricted options of the very  circumstance or of imprisonment and  simultaneously under endow the state  with responsibility for the fate of  those within its sphere of life and  death control without such a commitment  we will have really eviscerated the  meaning of habeas corpus it is the  responsibility of police to use least  force possible to deescalate passions  not inflame them to distinguish among  guilty and innocent adult and distressed  it is not an easy job particularly in a  society as well armed as ours  but it points to the necessity of a  politics that is beyond cost-cutting or  carelessly probabilistic profiling or  throwing bigger and better weapons at an  already inflamed social setting and  there is a terrible familiarity a  repetition to these crises the  incoherent policies of bunkering and  quarantine the criminalization of whole  populations the militarized purported  necessity of human degradation the  bloody splashing repercussion of  retributive justice and so the witnesses  scatter the chickens come home to roost  and I remain curious about the explosive  histories that sent those chickens  flying into distant disorder but that we  assign to oblivion as they now now glide  back to earth like embers and ashes the  regrouping phoenixes of reiterated to  traumas I'm left with an ominous vision  of flocks of mean-spirited birds  broken-winged and brooding gathering in  unnatural silence as though before a  storm  my next to the last story number seven  and this was actually the subject of a  column I did for those of you who read  my column about going out to dinner in  Manhattan to a nice little Japanese  restaurant on the Upper West Side and  around 5:30 in the afternoon the place  was filled with mothers and fathers  after school kids with their books  spread out of me amid this platters of  sushi and right in the middle of the  miso soup course the place was robbed  and too skinny teenagers probably no  more than 15 years old had come in and  when the waitress turned her back seized  the cashbox and fled with five staff  members pounding after them to no avail  it was very sudden it was very unnerving  and I was left with a kind of fairy tale  lack of resolution fists were shaken no  one died life continued shaken but not  substantially stirred and police weren't  even called because it's New York  and because the maitre d explained with  a shrug that there wasn't a lot of cash  is a mostly credit card of establishment  and in the truly only in New York form  people went back to their meals and  children went back to their homework and  the place resumed the murmurs of  conversation and clinking crockery and  next to me there was an understandably  distressed mother she was at a table in  the throes of the adrenaline everyone in  the establishment was suffering and she  lashed out at those horrible people but  the longer she went on the more it  became clear that the plural as she used  it was broader than this just the two  kids who had come and gone her  indictment of those and of people was of  some vastly expanding plural some big  bang repercussion of universal  horribleness the object of whom extend  far beyond the to extended far beyond  the two skinny kids who ran off with the  till but that wasn't the hard part the  hard part was listening to her child for  therein lies the hinge of the next  generation this child of only five or  six began to muse aloud about what he'd  do to bad guys if he ever caught them  the little boys piping voice carried  well as he really was quite gleefully  as little kids do imagining all the  things he'd do if he ever caught the  robber or a robber and it was vivid and  astonishingly graphic I assume he picked  it up from video games but honestly his  little rant reminded me nothing so much  as two cultural influences first the  kind that little girls are subject to  who are already so conflicted about  their body image that the first thing  they do with the Barbie doll is cut off  her hair and then snap off her head the  second cultural reference that came to  mind was that of our great and iconic  jurist the right honourable William  Blackstone who was so eloquent on the  subject of drawing and quartering from  his 18th century commentaries he  described drawing which means putting on  a rack and stretching the bodies of all  the joints are dismembered and then  quartering which requires that the body  be sawed into at least four pieces and  that then the forgotten parts of the  ritual isn't draining the blood  splitting heads putting heads on spikes  and so forth anyway the sound and the  fury and the details that this child of  this child imagined tactics of tore  terror of torture had a resonance that  seemed delivered across centuries and so  it was that it stayed with me awhile and  it stayed with me while I finished my  meal and then walked up the street two  blocks to a book party for Nell  Bernstein and her new book burning down  the house and her book is about juvenile  detention in the United States where  children and young teens are thrown into  prisons and become the objects of all of  that little boys elaborate fantasies  though that little boy wasn't old enough  to imagine the degree of sexual assault  in real juvenile facilities he hasn't  yet imagined all the details like the  kids sent to solitary for months at a  time or kneeling on glass for 12 hours  at a time or if they soil themselves  they have to clean it up themselves  afterwards as a general ethic of  violence and torture in general and in  juvenile prisons by and large that  little boy in the restaurant had pretty  much inhaled the cultural narrative of  racial racialized punishment danger  dehumanization and the need to make  these horrible people  as his mother put it disappear and as I  sat at this party and listened to Nell  Bernstein and realized that she was  recounting exactly what this little boy  and the restaurant had dreamed of doing  to those people I reflected upon the  fact that these are real bodies upon  whom that retribution is is enacted from  whom the imagined price is paid these  are real children who are the children  of the nannies that abound all over  Manhattan they are children who've grown  up in foster care or who have emotional  problems of themselves been victim of  crimes they are abused kids kids who've  been raped and trafficked but sometimes  they are just kids who haven't done much  of anything wrong their kids from  so-called million-dollar blocks for  police concentrate all their superpowers  their kids with arrest warrants issued  for riding bikes on sidewalks or having  a dusting of marijuana on their person  those whose scuffles as Nell Bernstein  puts it on the basketball court lead not  to being benched but rather to a month's  worth of solitary confinement they are  miscreants mischief makers children with  special needs one of the women at the  book party asked an astonishment isn't  this illegal what guards do to kids  who've been locked up and now responded  of course and when those kids who were  in there were having a joint get beaten  up and raped by their keepers they  learned that it really isn't about what  they have done it's all about who they  are so who are they what is going on in  our heads and I'll end with my eighth  story the story I call the woman who  fell from the sky and this is a story  about stories it's about the degree to  which race is an overwhelmingly  capacious proxy for narratives of  intellect  and criminality and sexuality and  economics and health and geography free  will fear of failure and the will to  keep on going it is a multi light story  that I pose in juxtaposition to the  story with which I began of the million  and a half black men who have  disappeared even though this is about  the body of a woman a woman who  committed suicide by leaping out a  window with her 10 month old baby  strapped strapped to her chest in one of  those snuggly little baby bjorn type  carriers and the first story I read was  this headline in the New York Post baby  survives Harlem mother's death leap  because you can always rely on the New  York Post for honest concision there is  just so much packed into those few words  now just the word Harlem instantly  shapes assumptions about what happened  she must be black desperately poor a bad  mother probably on drugs nuff said end  of story  and you can imagine the online  commentary to precisely that effect the  next day however the New York Daily News  coming a little late to the story ran  the report on its website under this  headline Manhattan mother jumps eight  stories to her death with her infant son  strapped to her chest baby survives with  minor injuries  with Manhattan as the modifier this  mother was more likely to be seen in  online commentary as white and as  selfish as desperately ambitious and not  on enough drugs for what was assumed to  be postpartum depression now as it  turned out this mother was also an  employee of the Manhattan State Supreme  Court and a graduate of Columbia Law  School  of course there was a stereotyped  narrative for that as well  glenn beck's news site the blaze  reported the story as lawyer mother  leaps from eight story building and  commentary from readers was peppered  with surmise about whether the woman was  a feminazi with a liberal death wish and  with relief that there was one less  lawyer in the world and finally three  days later there was this headline which  straddled the question of identity was a  bit of ambiguity  sources say Harlem lawyer Cynthia Walken  Haim penned a 13-page handwritten  suicide note before jumping out of an  eight-story window taking her son Kesten  with her the baby was saved from the  impact when the woman landed on her back  on the pavement and commentators were  pretty confused about this one harlem  lawyer lost named Walken Hine son with a  funny Ebonics sounding name like Kesten  and they were quite enraged  anyway I saved all of the headlines  about this incident and there were more  and turns them into this PowerPoint that  I shared at a conference not long ago  and an audience member remarked that she  was just blown away by the story she  said that it was beyond tragic such a  terrible mystery as to why all these  women were jumping off buildings with  babies strapped to their chests to say  nothing of the fact that all the babies  survived every single time anyway I tell  you all of this because I am so  intrigued by the kinds of progression  that these headlines reproduce there was  just to reassure you only one woman  jumping out of her 8th floor window yet  eight levels of degradation eight  stories of dying this mother falling  over  and over through multiply headlined  categories of being descending through  multiple ontological layers to her  inescapable doom  Cynthia Walken Haim Harlem I'd and  Manhattan I'd accomplished lawyer and  Columbia Law School graduate depressed  wife and bad mother became a floating  signifier in death more than one being  in our cultural narrative torn into  pieces by powerfully loaded descriptors  in the mouths of others and I wish there  were some kind of bum in writing about  the pain of that incident I so so wish I  could will into being another ending but  this is not an easy world we live in  very vexed times on an exhausted planet  where more and more are and will be  subject if you were in fewer of life's  necessities like clean water air enough  to eat  shelter and kindness it is sometimes  very hard to find the inspiration and  resilience necessary to expand and  contract in the face of all these  dismembering social forces and I was  very moved at lunch as many of you  students and faculty spoke of the  university as a place to think in  collective ways about our collective  problems about not losing ourselves  about providing stewardship about  knowing that the world will be a cruel  run at times so hurting ourselves as the  airlines tell us by donning our  parachutes putting on your oxygen masks  putting on stilts to rise above it all  donning our high-heeled running sneakers  and emotional sponges crash helmets and  pillowed landing gear so when challenged  by walls when thrown off cliffs we can  learn to become spider-man and Wonder  Woman prepared as well as gifted with  supernatural powers  we so need these gathered powers of  thought  we must continue to provide the cushions  from full-body blows the softer landings  the gentler understandings weaving the  safety net that will sustain all of us  our babies our boys our girls our  parents our politicians and our police  so that they might all face this world  with resilience and kindness and when  thrown for a loop or from a great height  we might bounce back up unbroken and  carry on
 

